Objective
Rebuild the Volt Velocity presentation layer so it works correctly on desktop, tablet, mobile portrait, and mobile landscape.
Do not rebuild the gameplay mechanics. Keep the existing Three.js game, ship logic, track logic, multiplayer, power-ups, health, shield, lap, position, speed, competition data, and backend state.
The current problem is structural: the HUD behaves like a desktop interface that has been resized. The target is a true responsive racing-game HUD where the road and ship remain the visual focus.
1. Core Rule
Do not scale the current layout.
Create four presentation modes:
1. Desktop
2. Tablet
3. Mobile Portrait
4. Mobile Landscape
Share the same game state and mechanics, but change:
- HUD composition
- controls
- camera framing
- utility menu
- spacing
- typography
- safe-area behavior
2. Main Current Problems
The current build has:
- too much HUD in the upper-left
- too many stacked cards in the upper-right
- oversized desktop touch controls
- too much vertical space used by the bottom control area
- a large minimap/circuit block
- server connection UI taking central space
- scattered audio/pause/settings/fullscreen controls
- portrait and landscape layouts that are too similar
- control panels that compete with the road
- poor use of mobile screen width and height
The center of the screen must remain gameplay-first.
3. Recommended Architecture
<GameViewport>
  <GameCanvas />

  <ResponsiveHUD
    deviceMode={deviceMode}
    orientation={orientation}
  />

  <ResponsiveControls
    deviceMode={deviceMode}
    orientation={orientation}
  />

  <GameUtilityMenu />
</GameViewport>
Use:
type DeviceMode =
  | "desktop"
  | "tablet"
  | "mobile-portrait"
  | "mobile-landscape";
Do not position every component with unrelated top, left, right, and bottom values.
Create reusable HUD zones.
4. Game Viewport
.gameViewport {
  position: relative;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  min-height: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  background: #020817;
}

.gameViewport canvas {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  width: 100% !important;
  height: 100% !important;
  display: block;
}

.gameViewport.isFullscreen {
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0;
  width: 100dvw;
  height: 100dvh;
  z-index: 9999;
}
Use 100dvh, not only 100vh.
Listen to:
- viewport resize
- window.visualViewport
- orientation changes
- fullscreen changes
